yet still retain essentially all the signal, as seen in Fig. 4.5 (a) and (c).

The other isolation requirement is imposed to reduce jet-like events, and uses a larger area than the previous 4\(\times\)4 cluster isolation condition. The sum of transverse energies over this larger area, \(E_{T}^{\Delta R<0.7}\), is defined as the sum of all BEMC and EEMC tower \(E_{T}\) and TPC track \(p_{T}\) within a cone radius of \(\Delta R~{}\equiv~{}\sqrt{\Delta\eta^{2}+\Delta\phi^{2}}~{}<~{}0.7\) around the candidate track. The \(e^{\pm}\) candidate track is excluded from the sum of TPC track \(p_{T}\) to avoid double-counting the candidate's energy in the \(E_{T}^{\Delta R<0.7}\) sum. Figure 4.6 shows a diagram of the \(e^{\pm}\) candidate and the region that is summed in this cone around the \(e^{\pm}\). This quantity \(E_{T}^{\Delta R<0.7}\), known

