as the "near-side cone sum," is used to form the ratio \(E_{T}^{e}/E_{T}^{\Delta R<0.7}\) which is required to be greater than 0.88. The distribution of this isolation ratio is shown in Fig 4.5 (b) and (d) for the data and \(W\to e\nu\) MC, respectively. To maintain a high efficiency, use of this larger area necessitates using a slightly looser condition on this isolation ratio than the tight condition on the \(E_{T}^{e}/E_{T}^{4\times 4}\) ratio However, even with this lower value for the ratio condition, a large fraction of the jet-like background events are removed by this requirement.

