#### \(Z\) Efficiency Correction Factors

Similar to the \(W\) efficiency correction, the total \(Z\) efficiency correction is factorized into four conditional efficiency terms described in this section: trigger, vertex finding, tracking, and algorithm efficiency, that is

\[\epsilon_{Z}^{tot}=\epsilon_{Z}^{trig}\cdot\epsilon_{Z}^{vert}\cdot\epsilon_{Z}^{ trk}\cdot\epsilon_{Z}^{algo}, \tag{6.6}\]

As described previously, the fiducial cross section will be measured within the kinematic acceptance of \(70~{}<~{}m_{e^{+}e^{-}}~{}<~{}110\)\(\mbox{GeV}\!/c^{2}\), \(E_{T}^{e}~{}>~{}15\) GeV, and \(|\eta_{e}|~{}<~{}1\). Therefore, each efficiency term is calculated for events that satisfy these acceptance requirements.

##### \(Z\) Trigger Efficiency

The trigger efficiency for \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) events, \(\epsilon_{Z}^{trig}\), is defined as the fraction of \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) MC events which satisfy the simulated trigger condition, similar to the \(W\) trigger efficiency. In Fig. 6.14 the trigger efficiency is plotted as a function of invariant mass (\(m_{e^{+}e^{-}}\)) and the ZDC coincidence rate of the zerobias event in which the simulated \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) pythia event was embedded. As expected, the \(Z\) trigger efficiency is higher than that of the \(W\) trigger efficiency, as there are two high-\(p_{T}\)\(e^{\pm}\) which can satisfy the trigger condition. Thus, even if one of the \(e^{\pm}\) falls outside of the detector acceptance due to a displaced vertex, or traverses a BEMC tower dead region, the trigger condition may still be satisfied by the other \(e^{\pm}\). The average \(Z\) trigger efficiency was estimated to be 0.968 \(\pm\) 0.004 \(\pm\) 0.005. The relative systematic uncertainty in the trigger efficiency was determined by varying the BEMC tower gains by their scale uncertainty and then calculating the variation in the trigger efficiency, which was found to be 0.5%.

### \(Z\) Vertex Finding Efficiency

Similar to the \(W\) vertex finding efficiency, the \(Z\) vertex finding efficiency, \(\epsilon_{Z}^{vert}\), is defined as the fraction of events with a reconstructed vertex that satisfied the cuts described in 4.1, where the reconstructed vertex is required to be within 5 cm of the simulated vertex from pythia. This was determined from the sample of \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) MC events which satisfied the simulated trigger condition, so the trigger efficiency had already been accounted for. The average value for the vertex finding efficiency is 0.938 \(\pm\) 0.006. In Fig. 6.15 the vertex finding efficiency is plotted as a function of \(m_{e^{+}e^{-}}\) and the ZDC coincidence rate. As expected, \(\epsilon_{Z}^{vert}\) does not depend on \(m_{e^{+}e^{-}}\); however, a dependence on ZDC rate is seen (similar to that of the the \(W\) vertex finding efficiency) due to the same pile-up related issues. It is also worth noting that the vertex finding efficiency is larger for \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) events than for \(W\to e\nu\), again due to the fact that there are two \(e^{\pm}\) in the \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) final state, which allows a vertex to be reconstructed even if one of the two \(e^{\pm}\) tracks is not reconstructed in the TPC.

#### Tracking Efficiency

The tracking efficiency, \(\epsilon_{Z}^{trk}\), for the \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) signal is determined from the subset of \(Z\to e^{+}e^{-}\) MC events which satisfy the simulated trigger condition, and have a valid reconstructed

